-- REFUSED (M4, spec §8.8): `WHEN JUST ""` on a `MAYBE STRING`.
--
-- M4 ruled payload-VALUE matches IN, on one recorded condition: "`WHEN JUST
-- <value>` must NOT compile to a presence test." A `MAYBE STRING` rides as a
-- plain optional text field, and its NOTHING encoding IS the empty string —
-- `<var> == ''`. So `WHEN JUST ""` is the one literal for which that condition
-- cannot be honoured: it lowers to exactly the absence test, and the interview
-- would answer NOTHING and JUST "" the same way.
--
-- Worse than conflation, and this is why it refuses rather than warns. The
-- module's own DA-MAYBE-STRING advisory declares the erasure direction — "an
-- empty submission is read as NOTHING" — so the only interview state the test
-- can reach is the state L4 calls NOTHING. The emitted answer would have been
-- INVERTED, not merely ambiguous, on the one input that reaches it.
--
-- Every NON-empty literal is fine: `WHEN JUST "lost my job"` lowers to
-- `c.the_stated_reason == 'lost my job'` and is exact. So is binding the
-- payload and testing it in the arm body.

DECLARE Claim
    HAS
      @desc The reason the claimant gave, if any
      `the stated reason` IS A MAYBE STRING

@export whether the claim needs review
GIVEN c IS A Claim @desc The claim under assessment
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`needs review` c MEANS
    CONSIDER c's `the stated reason`
    WHEN JUST "" THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

#EVAL `needs review` OF Claim WITH `the stated reason` IS NOTHING
-- FALSE (and `JUST ""` is TRUE — the two L4 answers this lowering cannot tell
-- apart)
